{'status': 'success', 'count': 1, 'memories': [{'id': 'mem_001', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'TopicState: Research directions in polymer deep learning and active hypotheses regarding interpretable physics-inspired neural network architectures. Currently restricted to existing polymer datasets, but leveraging validated hypotheses from other molecular deep learning tasks is permitted.', 'keyword': 'TopicState', 'content': {'research_goal': 'Accumulate knowledge and experience through continuous learning and practice to produce top-tier research in the field of polymer deep learning.', 'research_direction': ['With a total of 7 days available, dedicate the first half to property prediction tasks and the latter half to gradually attempting pre-training', 'Use Tg dataset as a sandbox, prioritizing physics-inspired deep learning models and testable mechanistic interpretability to gain general polymer deep learning insights and methodologies.', 'Directly implement and test novel model architectures, skipping standard benchmarking, but still comparing results against baselines.', 'Analyze the reasons for model success or failure based on experimental results to decide whether to further optimize details on the current basis or open up new model architecture directions.'], 'benchmark and datasets': ['Prioritize the Tg dataset to establish SOTA. Reserve PI1M strictly for pre-training and avoid premature usage.', 'Training & Evaluation Protocol: The Tg dataset is split 72/8/20 for train/val/test, applies early stopping (patience=30), and selects the best model by validation RMSE for final Testing.', 'Tg: 7170 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Glass Transition Temperatures. Baseline: rmse=50.5 by GAT, SOTA: rmse=30.8 by PerioGT', 'PI1M(Unlabeled Dataset for Pre-training): 1 million unlabeled one repeat unit p-SMILES.'], 'active_hypotheses': ['These hypotheses carry uncertainty; as knowledge and experience accumulate, they should be continuously revised to improve the quality of the hypothesis space.', 'Data_Centric_Foundation: Conduct a detailed dataset analysis to form a comprehensive dataset profile. Construct multimer data and baseline models, and construct 3D structural data and baseline models. For graph representation learning, perform a benchmark analysis on the impact of node and edge feature dimensions on model performance. This lays a solid and comprehensive foundation for subsequent research.', 'Advanced Algorithm Improvement: Improve current state-of-the-art molecular property prediction algorithms, such as DMPNN, Graph Transformer, and Long Short Range Message Passing, based on a deep understanding of their underlying mechanisms, making them better align with algorithmic or physical prior assumptions to achieve better results.', 'Self-Supervised Pretraining: Fully utilize multimer data and 3D structural data to conduct monomer versus multimer contrastive learning or 2D-3D contrastive learning, embedding polymer prior features into network weights to achieve better results.'], 'daily_journal': [{'day': 3, 'journal': "**Day 3 Journal: Paper Learning and IAPT Design**\n\n**Papers Selected and Analyzed:**\nSelected 2 papers following MetaMethod criteria:\n1. **MBNN-att (2024)**: Many-Body Function Corrected Neural Network with Atomic Attention - introduces softmax attention normalization for intensive properties. Key insight: Tg is an intensive property (temperature), and standard sum/mean pooling assumes extensive properties, potentially causing systematic bias. Attention weights are physically interpretable as atomic orbital contributions.\n2. **CELLI (2024)**: Charge Equilibration Layer for Long-range Interactions - generalizes classical Qeq into model-agnostic building block for equivariant GNNs. Enables modeling of long-range electrostatic interactions through explicit charge predictions.\n\nBoth papers saved to memory (mem_081, mem_082).\n\n**Experiment Designed (Not Executed):**\nDesigned Intensive-Aware Polymer Transformer (IAPT) architecture integrating Days 1-3 insights:\n- **Attention-based pooling** (MBNN-att): KEY innovation - softmax normalization for intensive Tg property\n- **Charge equilibration layer** (CELLI): Long-range electrostatic effects for polymer chains\n- **Monomer conformer features** (Day 1): 3D geometric features (bond lengths, angles, dihedrals)\n- **Max-aggregation over 3 RUs** (GRIN/Day 2): Repetition-invariance\n- **Periodic edges** (PerioGT): Polymer periodicity representation\n\n**Execution Status: FAILED**\nAll execution attempts blocked by persistent API rate limits (Error 429). Made 15+ attempts over ~30 minutes. Experiment design saved to mem_083 for future execution.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n1. MBNN-att's softmax attention provides principled aggregation for intensive properties - directly addresses Day 1's systematic bias (over-predict low Tg, under-predict high Tg)\n2. CELLI's charge equilibration captures long-range interactions beyond local GNN cutoffs\n3. Both papers provide transferable inductive biases applicable to polymer Tg prediction\n\n**Recommendations for Day 4:**\n1. Execute IAPT experiment when API available - most promising architecture integrating all insights\n2. Alternative: implement experiment code manually to bypass API\n3. Target: Beat SOTA RMSE 30.8 (current best: 34.14 from Day 1)"}, {'day': 4, 'journal': "**Day 4 Journal: IAPT v2 Experiment - Validated Insights, Failed to Beat SOTA**\n\n**Papers Selected and Analyzed:**\nSelected 4 papers following MetaMethod criteria (2 from pool, 2 from arXiv):\n1. **Physical Pooling Functions** (arXiv 2207.13779): Pooling function selection should match property type—sum for extensive (size-dependent), mean/max for intensive. Tg is intensive.\n2. **Equivariant Benchmark** (arXiv 2008.08461): Equivariant layers provide 23% error reduction vs 4% from depth—parameter-efficient architectural innovation.\n3. **ENINet** (pool): Line graphs capture many-body equivariant interactions at triplet level, addressing vanishing directional information.\n4. **SimPoly** (pool): Polymer-specific MLFF achieving Tg MAE=43K; inter-chain interactions essential; cutoff ~6.5Å required.\n\nAll papers saved to memory (mem_086-089).\n\n**Experiment Conducted:**\nExecuted IAPT v2 (Intensive-Aware Polymer Transformer) integrating Days 1-4 insights: attention-based pooling for intensive Tg property, L1 equivariant features, line graph for angular relationships, monomer conformer features. Ran comprehensive ablation study with 6 variants.\n\n**Results:**\n- **Test RMSE: 35.08** (5-seed ensemble) vs **SOTA 30.8** (PerioGT)\n- **Gap: 4.28 RMSE points** — FAILED to beat SOTA\n- Day 1 result (34.14) remains best\n\n**Ablation Results:**\n| Variant | RMSE | Key Finding |\n|---------|------|-------------|\n| Mean pooling | 37.89 | WORST — validates intensive property insight |\n| Max pooling | 35.35 | Good |\n| Attention pooling | **35.19** | BEST single model |\n| No equivariant | 36.76 | Removing hurts ~1.1 RMSE |\n| No line graph | 36.43 | Removing hurts ~0.5 RMSE |\n\n**Validated Hypotheses:**\n1. **Intensive property insight CONFIRMED**: Mean pooling worst for Tg (intensive), attention/max best\n2. **Equivariant features help**: ~1.1 RMSE improvement\n3. **Line graph helps**: ~0.5 RMSE improvement\n\n**Critical Issues:**\n1. **Conformer generation failure**: 83% failure rate for polymer trimers (unusual atom types Cd, Pb, Zn), losing 3D geometric information\n2. **Overfitting**: Train RMSE ~12-17 vs Val RMSE ~35-37\n3. **Missing pre-training**: PerioGT's SOTA uses PI1M pretraining—we haven't used this yet\n\n**Key Insight:**\nArchitecture innovations (intensive-aware pooling, equivariant features, line graphs) are validated but insufficient. **Pre-training on PI1M is likely the critical missing piece**—PerioGT achieves 30.8 via pretraining.\n\n**Recommendations for Day 5:**\n1. Pre-training on PI1M dataset (critical missing piece)\n2. Fix conformer generation: use monomers instead of trimers\n3. Address overfitting with better regularization\n4. Consider hierarchical architecture for multi-scale polymer structure"}, {'day': 5, 'journal': '**Day 5 Journal: Pre-training Experiment - Hypothesis Disproven**\n\n**Papers Selected and Analyzed:**\nSelected 4 papers following MetaMethod criteria (2 from pool, 2 from arXiv):\n1. **GPIP** (pool): Geometry-enhanced SSL with 3 tasks (masking, denoising, contrastive learning) achieving 10x data efficiency\n2. **MoCL** (arXiv): Domain knowledge-aware contrastive learning with bioisostere substitution for semantics-preserving augmentations\n3. **GraphMAE** (arXiv): Masked graph autoencoding with Scaled Cosine Error, SOTA on MoleculeNet transfer learning\n4. **GNN-SSM** (pool): Unified theory of GNN pathologies (gradient vanishing, over-smoothing, over-squashing) with state-space model solution\n\nAll papers saved to memory (mem_092-095).\n\n**Experiment Conducted:**\nTested the Day 4 hypothesis that "pre-training on PI1M is the critical missing piece." Implemented GraphMAE-style masked autoencoding combined with MoCL-style domain-aware augmentations. Ran 2 iterations with 100K and 300K PI1M samples respectively.\n\n**Results: HYPOTHESIS DISPROVEN**\n| Iteration | Test RMSE | Target (SOTA) | Gap |\n|-----------|-----------|---------------|-----|\n| v1 (100K PI1M) | 35.60 | 30.8 | +4.8 |\n| v2 (300K PI1M) | 36.14 | 30.8 | +5.34 |\n\n**Best Day 5 result: RMSE 35.60** — worse than Day 1\'s 34.14. Pre-training did NOT help.\n\n**Key Findings (Negative Result):**\n1. **Pre-training alone insufficient**: GraphMAE-style masked feature reconstruction on PI1M did not improve Tg prediction\n2. **Objective mismatch**: SCE loss collapsed to 0.0003, suggesting reconstruction task misaligned with downstream Tg prediction\n3. **Overfitting persists**: Train RMSE ~22 vs Val RMSE ~35\n4. **Architecture > Pre-training**: PerioGT\'s SOTA (30.8) likely comes from periodicity-aware architecture (multimer graphs, periodic edges), not just pre-training\n\n**Recommendations for Day 6:**\n1. Focus on architecture innovations (implement PerioGT\'s periodicity-aware design) rather than pre-training\n2. Apply GNN-SSM insights for gradient flow and overfitting mitigation\n3. If pre-training, use property prediction as auxiliary task instead of pure reconstruction\n4. Fix conformer generation (83% failure rate) to enable 3D geometric features\n\n**Experience saved to memory (mem_097).**'}]}, 'connections': {}}]}

